# hui GRENADE vs legacy FLARE — the SILENT half of the pool-vs-pool
# contact class (14z-129).
#
# The twin of tests/replays/pyron/84_pyron_clash_type64.rpl, and it exists
# to record a NEGATIVE that is easy to misread. It is what 88's header asked
# for — "an opposing PROJECTILE to clash with (P2 doing a pool-object move
# into the mine), not a walking fighter" — and it WORKS: the contact is real.
# It just is not a crash control, and the reason is the map, not the rig.
#
# THE SHAPE: P1 Huitzil throws the 214+MP grenade (pool type 68) while P2
# Demitri throws a head-on Chaos Flare on the same frame. Six attempts at
# varied relative offsets and with three different Huitzil pool moves;
# ATTEMPT A (simultaneous, f3400) is the one that lands.
#
# BOTH SIDES POKED, early window only (the replay-80 rule):
# POKES="1400:ff8782:10;1450:ff8782:10;1500:ff8782:10;1400:ff8b82:01;1450:ff8b82:01;1500:ff8b82:01"
#
# MEASURED 14z-129 on the huitzil stage-6 vertical (08944a7e) and its
# no-thunk twin (bc33983c), GUARD_DEBUG=1:
#   WITH the thunk    2 probe fires at f3430-3431 — one at map index 0x44
#                     (68, the grenade) and one at 0x04 (the flare) — with
#                     A1=ffff9400 and A6=ffff9500, i.e. TWO POOL SLOTS.
#                     That is the pool-vs-pool contact the 37-leg census
#                     could not produce. END 5520.
#   WITHOUT it        END 5520 — NO CRASH.
#
# WHY NO CRASH, and why that is not a licence to drop the thunk: the
# consequence of over-indexing the vanilla 64-entry map is PER-INDEX.
# map[68] = 0x12 is survivable where map[64] = 0x4e (pyron's satellite) is
# the f7997 fault. HANDOFF's out-of-range doctrine: "no crash" clears a LOUD
# entry completely and a SILENT one not at all — so this leg says the
# huitzil grenade's over-index is SILENT, and says nothing whatever about
# whether it is safe.
